            "name": "web_search",
            "args": {
              "query": "prompt injection attacks authoritative sources"
            },
            "result": "[1] Prompt Injection | OWASP Foundation\nURL: https://community.owasp.org/attacks/PromptInjection\nSnippet: The core vulnerability that gives rise to prompt injection attacks lies in what can be termed the \"semantic gap\". This gap arises because both the system prompt (developer instructions) and the user's input (data or new instructions) share the same fundamental format: natural-language text strings.\n\n[2] Prompt injection - Wikipedia\nURL: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prompt_injection\nSnippet: Prompt injection is a cybersecurity exploit and an attack vector in which innocuous-looking inputs (i.e. prompts) are designed to cause unintended behavior in machine learning models, particularly large language models (LLMs).\n\n[3] 2. Prompt Injection (Direct / Indirect) | Microsoft Learn\nURL: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/security/zero-trust/catalog-ai-attack-techniques/prompt-injection\nSnippet: Describes direct and indirect prompt injection attacks against LLMs, why they succeed, and the controls needed to treat untrusted content as adversarial.\n\n[4] Prompt Injection Attacks: Examples and Defences\nURL: https://blog.cyberdesserts.com/prompt-injection-attacks/\nSnippet: 17 prompt injection examples with real attack payloads. Learn how direct and indirect attacks work plus defence strategies that reduce risk\n\n[5] Prompt Injection Attacks on Large Language Models: A Survey of Attack ...\nURL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/org/science/article/pii/S1546221826001384\nSnippet: Defense against prompt injection attacks, in particular, plays a crucial role throughout the entire AI system lifecycle. Prompt injection attack research aims to identify and defend against malicious input manipulation targeting large language models.",
            "success": true
          }
